From 9f60bb7c0173bc3bb6697b4372e2f989fc3d57c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 12:40:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] policy routing: WireGuard targets, IPv6, commit-confirm
 (closes the post-MVP backlog)

Closes backlog "Policy Routing - what is left after the MVP", all three parts:

1. WireGuard targets (WGC1-5, mark codes 6-10, tables wgc1-5, fail-closed
   like OVPN). The BE96U flow cache ignores an ip rule for a WireGuard
   egress; the fix ASUS shipped and VPN Director uses is the blog skip list
   (/proc/blog/skip_wireguard_network: a flow whose src or dst is listed is
   never offered to the accelerator). Fed exactly as VPN Director feeds it:
   a source selector contributes its CIDR, any other selector (destination
   set, MAC) needs the LAN skipped - VPN Director's "all LAN" case. That is
   a real cost (no HW acceleration for LAN-sourced flows while such a rule
   exists) so it is logged on apply and shown on the rule's chip. The list
   has no refcount and is shared with VPN Director and the WG server, so
   the script records which entries IT added (/tmp/reaper_pbr/skipnets)
   and removes only those on teardown. fc flush after a WG apply.
2. IPv6. Every rule now also emits into the ip6tables mangle chain
   (rwfw6_<obj> sets, guarded on the set existing; v6 source CIDRs; MAC on
   both families) with ip -6 rules in the same 9000/9100 bands. A tunnel
   table with no v6 routes sends the selected v6 traffic into the
   fail-closed prohibit, so a v4-only tunnel cannot leak its v6 half.
3. Commit-confirm (I4), the same shape as reaper_fw: reaper_pbr.cgi
   stages the draft in nvram RAM (never commits), rc arms a candidate with
   a cru-owned deadline (timer shared: reaper_fw_confirm) and a PRE-
   GENERATED rollback built from /jffs/reaper_pbr/lastgood; confirm is the
   only path that writes flash; boot prefers a complete lastgood snapshot
   and re-syncs nvram RAM to it. First boot on this build seeds lastgood
   from nvram so a rollback source always exists. The firewall re-hook
   re-runs the armed candidate inside a window. Page: Apply-and-confirm
   button, Keep/Revert card with the router-owned countdown, WireGuard
   1-5 in the target list, bypass/"client not enabled" chips, IPv6 note.

Verified on the host: the generated apply.sh under mock iptables/ip/ipset
(v4+v6 marks, ipset guard, both-family rules, skip-list bookkeeping
pre/new across apply, re-apply and teardown); the commit-confirm state
machine compiled against a fake nvram (boot seed, arm, second-arm refusal,
cron deadline rollback, confirm = 1 commit + lastgood, stray confirm no-op,
armed-flag-without-marker boot, disable via draft); page JS parses with
all 25 dicts; langcheck 0 breakers.
